# Operating it

## How it gets deployed

Pushing to `{{ .DefaultBranch }}` builds an image and publishes it to
`ghcr.io/{{ .Owner }}/{{ .Name }}`, tagged with the commit sha.

Tags are always explicit. The operator's admission webhook **rejects `:latest`
and untagged images**, so an image reference you cannot pin is one it will not
run. That is a feature: it means what is running can always be traced back to a
commit.

## Changing what runs

[`webapp.yaml`](https://github.com/{{ .Owner }}/{{ .Name }}/blob/{{ .DefaultBranch }}/webapp.yaml)
is the desired state, and it lives with the code so it can change through a pull
request:

```yaml
spec:
  image: {{ .Image }}
  replicas: {{ .Replicas }}
  port: {{ .Port }}
```

Point `spec.image` at a published tag and apply it:

```bash
make deploy
```

## Scaling

```bash
kubectl -n {{ .Namespace }} scale webapp {{ .Name }} --replicas=4
```

The **WebApp** tab on this component's page in Backstage follows that within a
few seconds.

To hand the replica count to an autoscaler instead, add an `autoscaling` block:

```yaml
spec:
  autoscaling:
    minReplicas: 2
    maxReplicas: 10
    cpuThresholdPercent: 70
```

When that block is present the operator stops managing the replica count and the
HPA owns it, so what you asked for and what is running will legitimately differ.
The WebApp tab shows both numbers rather than pretending they are one.
